  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 212 AM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nA weak disturbance will move through Saturday night into Sunday. The \nsfc low will track south into the Southern Plains and keep the bulk \nof the precipitation south of the forecast area. There is a low end \nchance that light precipitation could skirt the very southern \nportion of the forecast area, far southwest Nebraska, however even \nif light rain does occur, qpf values are expected to remain below a \nhundredth. The upper level ridge will continue to progress eastward \ninto early next week with an upper level trof moving in Wednesday. \nSoutherly flow will bring adequate moisture to the area ahead of the \ntrof, will see a chance for scattered to widespread precipitation \nchances on Wednesday into Wednesday evening. Temperatures will \nbe mostly in the 50s during the day and expect the precipitation\nto fall as rain, however, should precipitation linger into the \novernight, there could be a potential for a brief period of \nrain/snow mix across western Nebraska, as overnight lows dip \nnear 30 degrees. As for temperatures through the rest of the \nweek, high pressure should continue to keep temperatures warm \nthrough mid- week with highs in the 50s across all of western \nand north central Nebraska. \n\nAttention then turns to another disturbance moving in from the \nPacific NW, towards the end of the forecast period. At this time\nconfidence is low in the exact track, but is expected to move \ninto the High Plains/Central Plains region, bringing the next \nchance for precipitation. At this time high temperatures will \nremain mild in the 50s so expected precipitation to be in the \nform of rain for any daytime precipitation, but should precip\nlinger into the overnight, there will be the potential for\nrain/snow mix.",
